# React Best Practices

This document outlines React best practices for our project, focusing on component design, state management, performance optimization, and TypeScript integration.

## Component Design

- Use functional components with hooks instead of class components
- Follow the Single Responsibility Principle - each component should do one thing well
- Keep components small and focused (< 250 lines of code)
- Use composition over inheritance to reuse component logic
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
- Implement proper prop validation with TypeScript types

## State Management

- Use React Context for global state where appropriate
- Leverage React Query for server state management
- Implement useState and useReducer for local component state
- Keep state as local as possible to the components that need it
- Consider using Zustand or Redux Toolkit for complex global state

## Performance Optimization

- Use React.memo() for expensive renders when needed
- Implement useMemo() and useCallback() for computational/referential stability
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders by structuring state properly
- Use proper dependency arrays in useEffect and other hooks
- Split large components for better code splitting
- Implement virtualization for long lists (react-window or react-virtualized)

## Code Organization

- Group related components in feature directories
- Keep utility functions separate from component logic
- Use consistent naming conventions (PascalCase for components)
- Maintain clear separation between container and presentational components
- Store shared types in dedicated type files

## Testing

- Write unit tests for business logic and hooks
- Implement component tests with React Testing Library
- Focus on behavior over implementation
- Test edge cases and error states
